<h2>The charge structure</h2>
<p>FIR No. 320/2024, PS Shalimar Bagh, invoked a layered charge: Section 70(2) BNS (gang rape of a woman under eighteen), Section 123 BNS (causing hurt by poison or stupefying substance with intent to commit an offence), Section 3(5) BNS (common intention), and Sections 6 and 17 POCSO (aggravated penetrative sexual assault and abetment). For an accused whose alleged act was locking a door, the combination matters: common intention and abetment make the enabler answerable for the principal offence, and POCSO&rsquo;s Section 17 punishes abetment with the punishment provided for the offence abetted.</p>

<h2>The bail hearing&rsquo;s three battlegrounds</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Role minimisation.</strong> &ldquo;He did not rape her&rdquo; framed the applicant as peripheral. The Court reframed: locking the room from outside, aware that the prosecutrix was losing consciousness and that the co-accused &ldquo;did not have clean intentions&rdquo;, was itself the participation.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Knowledge.</strong> The defence said the drinks were consumed after the applicant left. The Court found even that version did not explain the locking — the act that mattered occurred with knowledge of her state.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Narrative gaps.</strong> The locked-room question was answered by the prosecutrix&rsquo;s own testimony that a named person opened it; a gap pressed in argument dissolved on the record.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why the trial stage cut against release</h2>
<p>The prosecutrix had been examined and had strongly supported the prosecution case — a factor that, in many bail matters, assists the defence by reducing tampering risk. Here it worked the other way: her unshaken testimony hardened the prima facie case, while the friend who accompanied her before the incident — a crucial corroborative witness — remained unexamined. The combination of gravity, supported testimony and pending crucial evidence led the Court to dismiss the application, with the order transmitted to the Jail Superintendent for communication to the accused.</p>

<h2>The wider pattern in POCSO bail practice</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Typical effect on bail</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Victim&rsquo;s testimony recorded and supportive</td><td>Strengthens prima facie case; release usually awaits other material witnesses</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Victim&rsquo;s testimony recorded and resiling</td><td>Most common gateway to bail in POCSO matters</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Scientific corroboration (FSL/DNA)</td><td>Weighs heavily against bail even for non-principal accused where common intention or abetment is charged</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Enabling role (lookout, door, logistics)</td><td>Treated as participation, not periphery, at the prima facie stage</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>For the defence:</strong> role-minimisation arguments need an innocent explanation for each specific act attributed — courts test conduct, not labels.</p>
  <p><strong>Watch the witness list.</strong> Identify which unexamined witnesses are genuinely &ldquo;crucial&rdquo;; once they depose, a renewed application stands on changed circumstances.</p>
  <p><strong>For complainants&rsquo; counsel:</strong> Delhi courts hear victim&rsquo;s counsel in POCSO bail matters as of course — a reasoned opposition addressing the specific role alleged is effective.</p>
</div>
